      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey quick question... im seeing these tiny little holes appearing all over my tomato leaves in the greenhouse. they're really small and hard to spot unless u look closely. i cant see any bugs actually crawling on them though. any idea what might be doing that??",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those tiny holes in your tomato leaves are almost certainly the work of **tomato hornworms** or **tobacco hornworms**, which are large, green caterpillars that often hide in the foliage. However, since you mentioned the holes are *tiny* and you can't see bugs, you might actually be dealing with **tomato leaf miners** (tiny white larvae that tunnel inside the leaf) or perhaps **thrips**, which are microscopic and leave silvery scars or tiny pinpricks rather than clean holes.\n\nIf it's **thrips**, they are often invisible to the naked eye, and you might need to use a strong magnifying glass or a flashlight to spot them moving on the underside of the leaves. They feed on the plant cells, creating those small, speckled holes you're seeing.\n\nTo confirm, you can try a simple trap: place a piece of white paper or a yellow sticky card near the plants; if you see tiny black specks or see the leaves curling up, it's likely thrips. If you see any larger green or brown caterpillars hiding in the leaf axils, then it's a hornworm.\n\n**Pro-tip:** If you suspect th",
